I am using IEEE floating points on this executable, but one of the Third
party libraries that I use requires me to send VAX floats.  So I convert
the IEEE floats to VAX floats before I pass floats to this library. 
This scheme is being used in many different applications I implemented
and it works.  However this particular API caused some problems.  I
spoke to the vendor, they confirmed the problem, and we're trying to
find out what is wrong.   

I use two libraries.  First one is also compiled with /IEEE option just
like my files.  
I wrote a simple test program to duplicate this problem.  I have tested
this on OVMS/AXP v6.2 and v7.1 they all produce same results.

The problem is: high and low order words are swapped when entered to the
function compiled without IEEE. 
If I don't use any conversion (assuming that RTL is trying to handle the
conversion itself)  Then the value I get inside the function is 1/4 of
the original value I passed.

I cannot pass pointers since I cannot modify this third party API.
There must be some kind of option to use to prevent this from happening
(some other APIs that requires vax floats works in the same setting that
I'm trying to do here, of course I don't know how they do it...)

I'm sending the test program that duplicates the problem below.

Sorry about long listings and ravings here, I just need some help to be
able to understand the problem.

Thanks a lot, any help would be greatly appreciated.

Ertugrul Akmese


---- start test5.c ----
This is the main routine which uses IEEE floats

#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <cvtdef.h>
#include <cvt$routines.h>

int testsub(float);

main()
{
int st,err;
float fval=50.0;
float ieeefval=50.0;


printf("main: Initial (%e:%#x)\n\n", fval, *((int*)&fval));  

err = testsub((float)fval);  /*try it without conversion */
    

st=CVT$CONVERT_FLOAT(&ieeefval, CVT$K_IEEE_S, &fval, CVT$K_VAX_F,
CVT$M_VAX_ROUNDING);
printf("main: ieeefval:%e:%#x  fval:%e:%#x\n\n", (double)ieeefval,
*((int*)&ieeefval), (double)fval, *((int*)&fval));    /* printout result
of conversion */

err = testsub((float)fval);  /* try it with converted values */

}

----- end test5.c -----

----- start testsub.c ----
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <cvtdef.h>
#include <cvt$routines.h>

int testsub(float value)
{
    float ieeefval=0.0;
    float fval=0.0;
    int st=0;    

    printf("  Initial: testsub(%e:%#x)\n", value, *((int*)&value));  /*
show passed float at the start */

    fval=value;    
    st=CVT$CONVERT_FLOAT(&fval, CVT$K_VAX_F, &ieeefval, CVT$K_IEEE_S,
CVT$M_ROUND_TO_NEAREST);
    printf("   =     : ieeefval:%e:%#x  fval:%e:%#x\n",
(double)ieeefval, *((int*)&ieeefval), (double)fval, *((int*)&fval));    
    
    memcpy((void*)&fval, (void*)&value, sizeof(float));    
    st=CVT$CONVERT_FLOAT(&fval, CVT$K_VAX_F, &ieeefval, CVT$K_IEEE_S,
CVT$M_ROUND_TO_NEAREST);
    printf("  memcpy : ieeefval:%e:%#x  fval:%e:%#x\n",
(double)ieeefval, *((int*)&ieeefval), (double)fval, *((int*)&fval));    
    
    printf("  Exiting: testsub(%e:%#x)\n\n", value, *((int*)&value)); /*
show it again */
    
    return 0;
}
---- end testsub.c ----


